From: Dani Eder <eder@blkbox.COM>
Date: Thu, 27 Jan 1994 17:42:03 -0600 (CST)
Subject: [#94-1-594] Are dome homes practical?

> I've always loved geodesic domes and the idea of living in one
> is intriguing but according to Stewart Brand, of Whole Earth
> fame, most early dome houses had a serious problem with water
> leakage. The reason, I suppose, is that you have all those edges
> and vertices to seal. Perhaps that's the reason they have such a
> poor resale value as was pointed out on this list. Domes are
> such a great idea there must be a way to make it practical. Does
> anyone know of recent progress in construction techniques ?
>                            

Regular suburban houses have lots of edges to seal also - the plywood
sheets used for roof sheathing are 4x8 feet, and most roofs are much
larger than this.  What the early dome builders didn't understand was
how important proper moisture protection is.  A standard roof has
3 layers of material over the plywood - a layer of roofing felt and
two layers of shingles - with the materials specifically laid to
shed water off the roof.  The early domes apparently were just
lumber frames with plywood filler triangles.  If you left a conventinal
house in this state, it would leak badly too.

