From: graps@clio.arc.nasa.gov (Amara Graps)
Date: Thu, 27 Jan 94 16:33:30 -0800
Subject: [#94-1-596] Product Contest

From: peb@procase.com (Paul Baclace):

>All this sounds like the AVS system with some plug-in applications to
>do GP/GA or data fitting.  It certainly is possible to compete with AVS
>since they charge a lot for their product ($2500, I think); sounds like
                                            ^^^^^ 
I think you may be off by a few thousand.  AVS is *very* expensive. (~$4-6K)
It's of a class of products that I call "graphical programming languages".
Icons represent modular pieces of code which are connected by "wires"
that show the direction that the data is flowing. Other programs that
perform similar functions (all on workstations) are: Data Explorer
(by IBM), Explorer (by SGI), Khorous (freeware by New Mexico State U?).
These programs eat up gobs of memory and specialize in *visualizing* 
(rendering, slicing through 3D data sets etc.) the data _once it's generated 
somewhere else_. I see a big hole in the market for similar programs that 
can actually *number-crunch* data and then visualize it at a final step.

I like the idea of this approach alot because I number-crunch data all
day long for atmospheric and astrophysical scientists, and this approach
is very clean and elegant. A visual program is also easy to show the
scientists how to use. And since it's extensible, you can write your own
customized modules and plug it into the system (I've heard, though, that
it's a very nontrivial task to do this with AVS and Explorer).

But what about the Mac market? (which is what Vince's product contest
was all about)

There isn't alot of good scientific software out there for Macs. I've ended 
up writing my own custom scientific software in Pascal, Basic and Fortran
but the user interface is usually minimal (although I have a slick
suite of programs with nice GUI's that I wrote for a group of infrared
astronomers a few years ago).

Apple has developed, during the last 5 years, something called Scientist's
Workbench. It looked similar to the above Graphical Programming Language
programs, but it was geared to perform number-crunching as well. The latest 
version of Scientist's Workbench was written by our own "crank up the voltage"
co-conspirator  Vince Kerchner.  I wrote the Programmers and Users Manuals
for it last summer. This software has been demo'd at shows and the 
response has been outstanding, but it has always been a research project
and never a "make-it-into-a-product" project.

The underlying problem is that Apple doesn't know beans about how to develop 
and market scientific software. The only real scientist that they had in the 
whole  company was Rob Wolff, who suffered a severe heart attack last November.
Rob has fought uphill battles with them for years- the project has been
killed 3 times, and most of the rest of the time, it has stagnated in
one form or another.

So this is the background behind Vince's product contest. He's tired of
waiting for Apple to do something with it, and wants to strike out on his
own with a program that would still be useful for scientists, but not
work anything like Scientist's Workbench (to keep the lawyer packs away).

He's started coding on it, has a shell up and running, and it looks like
the final program will be a combination of what Nick Szabo, my friend
Eliot Young, and I have suggested. We think it will be successful.

