Date: Thu, 27 Jan 1994 19:05:17 -0600 (CST)
Subject: [#94-1-599] FUSION: Advanced Concepts

>    You know, the PLASMAK(tm) sounds really interesting, but I'm starting
> to think it's just another cold fusion. Do you have any more information
> on it besides the tidbits that Kuloc posts to sci.physics.fusion 
> (when attacking Tokamak's) I see little hope of the Tokamak ever being
> commercially successful or viable for space use.

Yes, I do have more information, unfortunately I've been moved twice
since I last used the material, so this is from memory.  Until June of
1993 I was in the 'Advanced Civil Space' group at Boeing.  One of the
things I was working on was the potential of advanced fusion for
interplanetary missions.  Ordinary chemical propulsion and even nuclear
thermal (NERVA) were inadequate in the sense that tens of billions
would be required for a Mars mission.  In the process, I read Koloc's
papers and his patent on the plasma kernal concept.  As far as I can
understand the physics, it makes sense.
> 
>     Are electrostatic concepts really being tested? I thought Tokamak's
> and megalaser based inertial confinement killed most of the funding for 
> alternative concepts or is it being tested with private university money 
> rather than DoE money?
> 
The key is 'most of the funding'.  The work is literally tabletop scale
(a 12 inch vacuum chamber), and is funded by DOE at the Univ. of
 Ill.  They have produced up to 10^6 neutrons/sec, and the variation in
neutron rates scale with current injection just like the theory says it
should.  The work is described in published papers in 'Fusion Technology'
(I think that's the journal name) over the last couple of years.

We're talking about a couple of post-docs and some grad students scale
project, not a multi-megabuck program.  There are two ways to confine
the electron cloud that in turn holds the positive nucleii.  The U. of 
Ill. experiments use a charged spherical screen inside the chamber,
with a voltage relative to the chamber wall.  The screen voltage sucks
the electrons injected by an electron gun into the center, they drift
through the center (the potential inside a speherical charge is constant),
out the other side of the screen, then get reflected by the voltage
difference between the screen and the chamber wall on the other side.

The electrons keep bouncing back and forth until they hit a screen
wire, thus a constant current from the electron gun is required to
keep up the charge.  Since the electrons mostly cross through the
center of the sphere (aside from some random components they pick
up from the initial electron gun beam spread and scattering off
each other and the screen wires), the density of electrons at the
center is higher than near or outside the screen.  This creates
a negative potential well that can be used to trap the nucleii.

The second approach, worked on by Bussard, involves the use of
a set of coils around the surface of a sphere to reflect the
electrons back to the center.  Here you lose electrons through
the parts of the field that are parallel to the electron
motion (thus no cross product force to act on the electron).
So here too, you have to keep injecting electrons with an
electron gun to keep up the charge distribution.  Otherwise
the concept works the same.  How hard it is to deflect tens of
kV electrons can be gauged by the fact that the CRT you are
probably looking at now deflects 20 kV electrons through a
large angle in the picture tube.

The magnetic coil machine was mostly built (it is about 2 meters
across), and a lot of computer modelling done, but as of a year
ago it was not going to be run, because DARPA (now ARPA) decided
that they had no business doing fusion research (the work got
started under the heading of advanced propulsion, since a
powerful, lightweight power source can be used for all sorts of
high-Isp propulsion for military satellite delivery).  Apparently
after a while the higher level, political people made the
decision not to fund it any more.  Until then, the lower level
technical people handing out the money at DARPA kept it going
because of the potential to revolutionize a capability that
the military needed, and that, after all, was DARPA's mission -
to pursue high risk, high payoff potential research.

I haven't kept up with it over the latter part of this year,
mostly because I don't have good access to a technical library
that gets the relevant journals.  I hope to soon (within weeks)
start to get abstract tracking from a service my employer
subscribes to (they are working hard to get email delivery
of abstracts on an automated basis - right now you call them
with a subject, they do a computer literature search, and FAX
you the results), then I can set up ongoing searches on
'fusion' 'life extension', etc. and keep current much more
efficiently than I have been.

