Subject: [#94-1-606] Product Contest

>From: graps@clio.arc.nasa.gov (Amara Graps)

> AVS is *very* expensive. (~$4-6K)
>It's of a class of products that I call "graphical programming languages".
> [...]  _once it's generated 
>somewhere else_. I see a big hole in the market for similar programs that 
>can actually *number-crunch* data and then visualize it at a final step.

This doesn't change the nature of my observation (although it was nice
that you described AVS for those who haven't seen it), because what
I really had in mind was...

>But what about the Mac market? (which is what Vince's product contest
>was all about)

Technology arbitrage occurs between platforms (SGI used to be the main
choice for rendering 3D graphics, now it can be done on PCs and Macs 
(quality and throughput considerations aside)).  This is something to 
consider when making a new product and it is possible mainly due to the
every increasing performance/price ratio.  The original vendor of the
product category gets locked into the older platform and is usually 
slow to port their product to newer platforms.  The OS (speaking loosely)
is also a platform: companies that made it big with programs that
run on DOS aren't necessarily the companies that own the same logical 
turf in MSWindows.  

> I see a big hole in the market for similar programs that 
>can actually *number-crunch* data and then visualize it at a final step.

Right; AVS's graphical function layout/connectivity UI is pretty good for
data flow control of rendering, but it is harder to accomplish this for
arbitrary programs.  Perhaps something like MathCAD, which really is a
spreadsheet with no grid and two passes for assignment and evaluation, could
be coupled with a AVS-like viewing system.  This would avoid the problem
of creating a visual language for arbitrary computation.


