Subject: [#94-1-611] LEGAL: PPL1 supports Mike Price's suggestions

Extropians,

My apologies: I posted this last night, but from a machine that was not
registered with the List software, so I had to repost it tonight:

-------------------------------------------------------------------------
Mike Price writes (RE Ray's suggestions):

> I strongly agree with your sentiments.  Which is why I support your idea
> of a no-holds-barred open discussion of the list justice system - with
> a mandatory prefix JUSTICE or LEGAL or whatever - if only Simon! would
> agree.  BTW the snap poll I conducted has reached 13-0 in favour of an
> open discussion.  I'll publish the final total later today.  As a member
> of PPL-A I don't know whether to laugh at the absurd way PPL1 is driving
> away customers or cry at the disrepute ExI (as PPL1 official backer) is
> being tarred with.

Okay, Mike, I agree.  If so many List members want to discuss legal matters,
I'll buy your suggestion.  Perhaps this will convince people that my
motivation has always been to improve the quality of the List, and not
to boss people around.  Not that good intentions excuse anything, but
I'd rather have people think of me as inept than as fascist.

So that there will be no contextual ambiguity:  I, Simon! D. Levy, head of PPL1,
hereby state my intention not to oppose discussion of legal/justice issues,
so long as the prefix LEGAL or JUSTICE is used.

> One of the problems, IMO, is that PPL1 _is_ seen as ExI's official
> mouthpiece and therefore acting as the List Management.  This gets
> people's backs up, triggers off folks' paranoia - perhaps quite
> justifiably - we don't know unless we discuss it!  

Again, I agree with Mike.  I have tried to separate my being on the Board
from my being head of PPL1, but I can see that I was not clear enough about
this intention.  I probably should not have mentioned the provisional status
of PPL's in the same message as my PPL1 announcement.  

> What I propose is that PPL1 should be (almost) the same as other PPLs. 
> All PPLs are equal - each has their own law for resolving intra-PPL
> disputes.  Each PPL's charter, in addition to it's own rules, subscribes
> to an inter-PPL charter or code which lays out how inter-PPL disputes
> are mediated.  PPL Heads that run amok (deviate from their own charter)
> lose members to other PPLs, via market forces.

At the risk of boring you, I agree again, except that I would eliminate the
"almost" -- I have no intention of trying to give PPL1 powers beyond those
of any other PPL.

>     [note: PPL1 is then just ExI's own contribution to the PPL
>     scene - with one difference - it is the default PPL until a
>     list members chooses otherwise (ie finds an another ExI-
>     recognised PPL that accepts them).]

Sounds okay to me.  In fact, I would go one step further.  Mike's message
makes it pretty clear to me that ExI should not be in the PPL business.
ExI should either allow PPL's, and act only as a framework for letting them do
their thing, or we should disallow PPL's entirely.  My attempt at a 
compromise seems to have blown up in my face.  Any old PPL can be the
default, I think.  I will remain head of PPL1, out of a desire not to leave 
my PPL members (whose ranks are thinning as I write) in the lurch.
If it becomes clear to me that my services are no longer required (i.e., that
the remaining PPL1 members don't care what PPL they belong to), I'll step
down.

> My own proposal for the inter-PPL charter is simplicity itself.  Anyone
> can file charges against anyone, but they are judged solely against the
> defendant's PPL charter or etiquette code.  A natural Schelling point
> I feel, although more complex solutions must be possible.

One last time: I agree.

